= Simulated reality — Simple =

'''Simulated reality''' is the hypothesis that the world we experience might be generated by an artificial simulation.

It is a philosophical hypothesis. There is no generally accepted scientific evidence showing that our universe is simulated.

== The basic idea ==

Imagine that a sufficiently advanced civilization could create a computer simulation containing beings with experiences like ours.

If those beings could not observe anything outside their simulated world, they might experience that world as ordinary reality.

This creates a question:

<pre>
Could an observer know whether their world is fundamental
or generated by another system?
</pre>

The question is related to older philosophical thought experiments such as dreams, Descartes's evil demon, and the brain-in-a-vat scenario.

== Bostrom's simulation argument ==

Nick Bostrom published a famous simulation argument in 2003.<ref name="bostrom">[https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9213.00309 Bostrom, N. (2003), “Are We Living in a Computer Simulation?”]</ref>

The argument says that at least one of three broad possibilities must be true:

# Civilizations like ours almost never survive long enough to become capable of running huge numbers of realistic ancestor simulations.
# Civilizations that become capable of doing this almost never choose to run many such simulations.
# If advanced civilizations do run enormous numbers of conscious ancestor simulations, simulated observers could greatly outnumber nonsimulated observers.

Under a particular way of treating oneself as a random observer, the third case would make being simulated more probable.

This is a conditional argument.

It does '''not''' prove that we are simulated.

Bostrom and Marcin Kulczycki later found a mathematical error in the original derivation and published two corrections that preserve the broad conclusion.<ref name="patch">[https://doi.org/10.1093/analys/anq107 Bostrom, N. and Kulczycki, M. (2011), “A Patch for the Simulation Argument,” ''Analysis'' 71(1), 54–61.]</ref>

== Why the probability is disputed ==

To calculate a probability, we would need answers to questions such as:

* Can a computer simulation be conscious?
* How much computation would it require?
* Would advanced civilizations actually run such simulations?
* Which observers should be counted?
* Should we treat ourselves as randomly sampled from that group?
* What prior probability should the simulation hypothesis receive?

These questions do not have established answers.

Brian Weatherson criticized the observer-sampling step in Bostrom's argument, and Bostrom later replied.<ref name="weatherson">[https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9213.00323 Weatherson, B. (2003), “Are You a Sim?”]</ref>

== Simulation is not virtual reality ==

virtual reality is a designed environment used by a person who normally exists in a known external world.

The simulated-reality hypothesis is stronger. It suggests that the observer may not have access to any deeper physical level.

A virtual world can still be meaningful and socially real even if it is implemented by computers.

That philosophical question is separate from whether our universe is simulated.

== Simulation is not a scientific model ==

Scientists use computer simulations to study climate, galaxies, fluids, disease, evolution, and many other systems.

A scientific simulation is a model.

Researchers ask whether its code correctly implements the model and whether the model represents its intended target well enough for the scientific purpose.<ref name="sep">[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/simulations-science/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Computer Simulations in Science.”]</ref>

Using simulations successfully does not show that reality itself is a simulation.

== What would count as evidence? ==

Evidence would need to discriminate between competing hypotheses.

For example, a specific simulation model might predict a measurable artifact that a nonsimulation model does not predict.

Then researchers could test for that artifact.

But a hypothesis that says “the simulator can make any observation appear” can explain every possible result. That makes it extremely difficult to test scientifically.

== “Glitches” are not enough ==

Unusual coincidences, memory errors, unexplained events, or surprising physical phenomena are sometimes called “simulation glitches.”

An unexplained event is not evidence for a simulation unless a simulation theory predicted it better than alternative explanations.

A strong test needs:

* a specific prediction;
* a measurement;
* an alternative model;
* replication.

== Does quantum physics prove simulation? ==

No.

Quantum mechanics contains discrete quantities and probabilistic measurement outcomes, but “discrete” does not mean “computer-generated.”

Likewise, mathematical laws of physics are not automatically computer code.

Evidence for an external simulator would require more than an analogy between physical laws and software.

== Consciousness matters ==

Bostrom's observer-counting argument assumes that sufficiently detailed simulated beings can have experiences.

That is not a settled scientific fact.

If consciousness depends on biological or physical properties that a simulation does not reproduce, the number of simulated conscious observers could be very different from the number of simulated agents.

The problem connects directly to [[Consciousness]] research.

== Possible versus probable ==

A useful distinction is:

<pre>
possible
≠ probable
≠ demonstrated
</pre>

A simulated world may be logically possible under some assumptions.

That does not tell us how likely it is.

And probability arguments do not substitute for direct empirical evidence.

